#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0cf53f9-5781-5380-809b-ea89525ff95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326439} {:answer/id #uuid "ece12859-35e4-5b1e-90a7-d39b6b4f2f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326440} {:answer/id #uuid "0c31177e-a508-56c1-a6c7-3bb21a5ba7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326441} {:answer/id #uuid "90aad3df-60f3-546c-b14f-df2ff03f3599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326442} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d42aa4-0b17-5fff-8211-7f9419b8a1bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c31177e-a508-56c1-a6c7-3bb21a5ba7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326441} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce1120dc-53cd-505d-8efa-af7d97ec23d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c8611a54-1200-5e47-8def-2e7361f092cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-20982" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-1$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)>0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-4{x}^{3}+4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{3}+4x & =0 \\\\ -4x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -4x(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-1,0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-12{x}^{2}+4,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =-8<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =4>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statement I is false. The second derivative is negative at $x=-1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false while statement IV is true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "632cc29c-24ac-5199-ace3-c4ebe736bf79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326446} {:answer/id #uuid "2cae57a4-1c98-55dc-883b-9c6bf2a596f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326447} {:answer/id #uuid "77755ba8-def3-5c1b-a91b-8a76933cff1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326448} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d02997-ea04-59fe-ad3a-8d5c0678d29f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326449} {:answer/id #uuid "03657456-da74-5805-a5f6-a75f5a4c927f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "632cc29c-24ac-5199-ace3-c4ebe736bf79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326446} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0bc78c7-6707-55ea-88a7-9bfae578b2d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "36b293da-b761-54d5-a1fa-c581da4bd74e" :question/math-academy-id "q-20983" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+24x.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(2)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(2)=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=2$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+24x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-24x+24.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12x-24.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =12\\cdot 2-24=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{′}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-24x+24 & =0 \\\\ 6(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=2.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=1,3$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =6(1)^{2}-24(1)+24=6>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =6(3)^{2}-24(3)+24=6>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative does not change the sign at $x=2$, we conclude that $f(x)$ has no relative extrema at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0d7f6c4-492c-5647-8c1b-3591f0f27fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303581} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ce5e96-8993-5aa8-bc53-0d0630c04716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303582} {:answer/id #uuid "e161399f-c327-5510-a9a3-de111d14051b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303583} {:answer/id #uuid "ad08725b-3681-51b8-aecb-52af410596c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303584} {:answer/id #uuid "a8147da4-a1f4-519d-962b-dc643ef43c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0d7f6c4-492c-5647-8c1b-3591f0f27fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303581} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb40351c-19e2-541d-80e2-bf69fe3ad102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5be071e3-c224-574a-92ed-72af7b4c80c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-21541" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-2{x}^{5/3}+{x}^{2/3}.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{5})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{5})<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=\\frac{1}{5}$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=\\frac{1}{5}$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\frac{1}{5}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=-2{x}^{5/3}+{x}^{2/3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2/3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-1/3}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-\\frac{20}{9}{x}^{-1/3}-\\frac{2}{9}{x}^{-4/3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{5}) & =-\\frac{20}{9}\\cdot {5}^{1/3}-\\frac{2}{9}\\cdot {5}^{4/3}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {5}^{1/3}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a698bda-6e9a-5e86-93ce-5485a0a270cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345759} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd79b53-cd56-5dfc-9979-b27d3d102d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345760} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4894a8-96a8-5034-a841-1ceacc27922c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345761} {:answer/id #uuid "e78b2263-c151-5f34-8c9f-69ee87eef710" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345762} {:answer/id #uuid "6718f0ba-1315-5099-8b1f-9dda88175b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfd79b53-cd56-5dfc-9979-b27d3d102d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345760} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa3728f-5676-5271-a84b-087affe49287" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58e760c4-f329-5e64-bec0-d19376ff7221" :question/math-academy-id "q-22011" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-6$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(-6)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-6$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify whether $x=-6$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=12{x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=36{x}^{2}-{x}^{4},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2}-{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}(x+6)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =-6,0,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-6,0,6.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=-6.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=72x-4{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=-6$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(-6)=432>0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is positive at $x=-6,$ so this critical point is a relative *minimum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=-6$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-6)<0\\,\\times $\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *minimum* at $x=-6\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbee870b-4a67-5ccb-b274-45fac627be6e" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Given Critical Point Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2f9fb7d-1b47-56ca-9dc4-21f64726f4e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345766} {:answer/id #uuid "db81d32c-97fd-5a6f-90d6-0cec9d1c1a75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345767} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4f430f-a020-5a09-90b9-3042fd43018c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345768} {:answer/id #uuid "33d84df6-752c-5b1e-bf98-6c7d80b66dc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345769} {:answer/id #uuid "1c74a4ee-dc9e-5f77-8ecb-9f24edddc9bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33d84df6-752c-5b1e-bf98-6c7d80b66dc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345769} :answer-field/id #uuid "48b03e45-4e65-5aa0-a4a6-c1500340df7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d1f3f815-5705-5ffe-8f6e-280bb7f8ac8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-22016" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+36x+12.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+36x+12,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-60x+36.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=36{x}^{2}+24x-60.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =36(1)^{2}+24(1)-60=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find other stationary points. Solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-60x+36 & =0 \\\\ 12({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-5x+3) & =0 \\\\ 12(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-3) & =0 \\\\ 12(x-1)(x-1)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-4) & =12(-4-1)^{2}(-4+3)=-300<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =12(0-1)^{2}(0+3)=36>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =12(2-1)^{2}(2+3)=60>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nAt $x=1,$  the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=1$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}]}
